{:edb/db-before-t 1672 :edb/db-after-t 1673 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534985 50 #inst "2026-10-08T13:20:45.072Z" 13194139534985 true] [17592186133615 1100 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=\\ln(2x+y)\\,\\mathbf{i}+ x^2e^{3y}\\,\\mathbf{j}$.\nEvaluate $\\nabla \\cdot\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(1,0)$." 13194139534985 false] [17592186133615 1100 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=\\ln(2x+y)\\,\\mathbf{i}+ x^2e^{3y}\\,\\mathbf{j}.$ \nEvaluate $\\nabla \\cdot\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(1,0).$" 13194139534985 true] [17592186133615 1145 "If\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}=P\\,\\mathbf{i}+Q\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nis a vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf F$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf{F}=\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F=\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇ \\cdot F &= \\frac{∂P}{∂x} + \\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\ln (2x + y)) + \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2}e^{3y}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{2x + y} + 3x^{2}e^{3y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf {F}$ at the point $(1,0)$, and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∇ \\cdot F)(1, 0) &= (2)/(2(1) + 0) + 3(1)^{2}e^{3(0)} \\\\\n&= 1 + 3 \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534985 false] [17592186133615 1145 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\,\\mathbf{i}+Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf F$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf{F}=\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F=\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+y))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{e}^{3y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2x+y}+3{x}^{2}{e}^{3y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf {F}$ at the point $(1,0),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(∇\\cdot F)(1,0) & =\\frac{2}{2(1)+0}+3(1)^{2}{e}^{3(0)} \\\\  & =1+3 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534985 true] [17592186133619 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044029/assets/d37f2a445986b2aae60603fed3bef1b480d8f049dfe17a0cf26325ac2eb167d8.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $\\mathbf F?$\n\n- $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F = 0$\n\n- $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F > 0$\n\n- $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F <                    0$\n\n- $\\mathbf F$ is solenoidal" 13194139534985 true] [17592186133619 1100 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $\\mathbf F$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2131/Source/Images/b5bfdb81e347992660d868824f19dfd3.png)\n\n1. $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F = 0$\n2. $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F > 0$\n3. $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F < 0$\n4. $\\mathbf F$ is solenoidal" 13194139534985 false] [17592186133619 1145 "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\, \\mathbf F = \\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F\n$$\n\ndenotes the divergence of the vector field $\\mathbf F$.\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $\\mathbf F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $\\mathbf F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P$:\n\n- If $\\mathbf F$ acts like a source at $P$, then $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F > 0$ at $P$.\n- If $\\mathbf F$ acts like a sink at $P$, then $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F < 0$ at $P$.\n- If $\\mathbf F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P$, then $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F =0$ at $P$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2131/Source/Images/a3948ed745c39ad63bafd542288743db.png)\n\nIf\n\n$$\n\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = 0\n$$\n\neverywhere in the domain of $\\mathbf F$, we say that $\\mathbf F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statements II and III are false. For any point $P$, the sum of the inward-pointing vectors equals the sum of the outward-pointing vectors. Therefore, $\\mathbf F$ acts neither like a source nor a sink.\n- Statement IV is true. Since $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = 0$ everywhere, the vector field $\\mathbf F$ is solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\"" 13194139534985 false] [17592186133619 1145 "Recall that $\\textrm{div}\\, \\mathbf F = \\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $\\mathbf F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $\\mathbf F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $\\mathbf F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- If $\\mathbf F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $\\nabla \\cdot  \\mathbf F > 0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $\\mathbf F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $\\nabla \\cdot  \\mathbf F <         0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $\\mathbf F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $\\nabla \\cdot  \\mathbf F =0$ at $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044029/assets/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = 0$ everywhere in the domain of $\\mathbf F,$ we say that $\\mathbf F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statements II and III are false. For any point $P,$ the sum of the inward-pointing vectors equals the sum of the outward-pointing vectors. Therefore, $\\mathbf F$ acts neither like a source nor a sink.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = 0$ everywhere, the vector field $\\mathbf F$ is solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\"" 13194139534985 true] [17592186133628 1100 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=(2x-y)^2\\,\\mathbf{i}+(3y-2z)^2\\,\\mathbf{j}+(x-2z)^2\\,\\mathbf{k}$. Evaluate $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(1,2,1)$." 13194139534985 false] [17592186133628 1100 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=(2x-y)^2\\,\\mathbf{i}+(3y-2z)^2\\,\\mathbf{j}+(x-2z)^2\\,\\mathbf{k}.$ Evaluate $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(1,2,1).$" 13194139534985 true] [17592186133628 1145 "If\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}=P\\,\\mathbf{i}+Q\\,\\mathbf{j} + R\\,\\mathbf k\n$$\n\nis a vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf F$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F = \\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf{F}=\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}+\\dfrac{\\partial R}{\\partial z}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇ \\cdot F &= \\frac{∂P}{∂x} + \\frac{∂Q}{∂y} + \\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂}{∂x}((2x - y)^{2}) + \\frac{∂}{∂y}((3y - 2z)^{2}) + \\frac{∂}{∂z}((x - 2z)^{2}) \\\\\n&= 4(2x - y) + 6(3y - 2z) - 4(x - 2z) \\\\\n&= 4x + 14y - 4z\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf {F}$ at the point $(1,2,1)$, and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndivF(1, 2, 1) &= 4(1) + 14(2) - 4(1) \\\\\n&= 28\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534985 false] [17592186133628 1145 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\,\\mathbf{i}+Q\\,\\mathbf{j} + R\\,\\mathbf k$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf F$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F = \\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf{F}=\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}+\\dfrac{\\partial R}{\\partial z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((2x-y)^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}((3y-2z)^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}((x-2z)^{2}) \\\\  & =4(2x-y)+6(3y-2z)-4(x-2z) \\\\  & =4x+14y-4z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf { F}$ at the point $(1,2,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F(1,2,1) & =4(1)+14(2)-4(1) \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534985 true] [17592186133799 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324852 13194139534985 true] [17592186133799 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324873 13194139534985 true] [17592186133799 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324901 13194139534985 true] [17592186133799 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324908 13194139534985 true] [17592186133803 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324845 13194139534985 true] [17592186133803 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324859 13194139534985 true] [17592186133803 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324866 13194139534985 true] [17592186133803 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324894 13194139534985 true] [17592186133803 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324936 13194139534985 true] [17592186133812 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324880 13194139534985 true] [17592186133812 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324887 13194139534985 true] [17592186133812 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324915 13194139534985 true] [17592186133812 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324922 13194139534985 true] [17592186133812 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324929 13194139534985 true] [17592186134293 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534985 true] [17592186134293 1100 "Given that $F = 2xy^{2}i + 4x^{2}yj$, what is $divF$?" 13194139534985 false] [17592186134293 1100 "Given that $F=2x{y}^{2}\\,i+4{x}^{2}y\\,j,$ what is $\\text{div}\\,F?$" 13194139534985 true] [17592186134293 1145 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{2}y) \\\\  & =2{y}^{2}+4{x}^{2} \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534985 true] [17592186324845 1017 #edb/ref 17592186324846 13194139534985 true] [17592186324845 1089 "q-105046" 13194139534985 true] [17592186324845 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534985 true] [17592186324845 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044029/assets/0bc0e75bb577ca37b89568e14a4e0d03da7ce4f4853c4e8c57d9b2df79897b13.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=0$\n- $∇\\cdot F>0$\n- $∇\\cdot F<0$\n- $F$ is solenoidal" 13194139534985 true] [17592186324845 1113 #uuid "ff6ab763-a1b1-539d-a53d-5454fde40d28" 13194139534985 true] [17592186324845 1145 "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044029/assets/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statements II and III are false. For any point $P,$ the sum of the inward-pointing vectors equals the sum of the outward-pointing vectors. Therefore, $F$ acts neither like a source nor a sink.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere, the vector field $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\"" 13194139534985 true] [17592186324846 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324847 13194139534985 true] [17592186324846 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324848 13194139534985 true] [17592186324846 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324849 13194139534985 true] [17592186324846 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324850 13194139534985 tr